<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Smart Fire Hydrant</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerEdge</data>
      <data key="d7">IoT smart fire hydrants that record acoustic signals (noises on the pipe network) daily. Signals are correlated between multiple fire hydrants. Part of a large-scale IoT network that also includes meters, pressure monitoring and water quality devices.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">API Gateway</data>
      <data key="d6">ApiGateway</data>
      <data key="d7">Receives the correlated acoustic files on a daily basis. Provides authentication and security; used instead of pushing directly to S3 because of the heavy focus on IoT security.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">S3</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Persists the acoustic signal files pushed through API Gateway.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">ECS</data>
      <data key="d6">ECS</data>
      <data key="d7">Daily batch process running as container microservices. Each file is processed as a node pair to look for correlations in the acoustic signals, including persistent correlations that occur over many days. Chosen over Lambda because the acoustic algorithms are very processor intensive.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">SageMaker ML Endpoint</data>
      <data key="d6">SageMaker</data>
      <data key="d7">ML endpoint that further classifies persistent correlations and assigns a probability (leak score). The model is retrained about every six weeks using feedback from the field so it improves over time.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">Analyst</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyAnalyst</data>
      <data key="d7">Human analyst team that receives the leak score, reviews the persistent correlation, talks with the water utility to rule out other nearby noise sources, and updates the leak status to field investigation required.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Correlated acoustic signal files are pushed through API Gateway on a daily basis.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">After authentication and security at the API Gateway layer, files are pushed and persisted in S3.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">A daily batch process kicks off in ECS where each file is processed as a node pair to look for correlations in the acoustic signals.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Persistent correlations (occurring over many days) are sent to the SageMaker ML endpoint to be further classified and assigned a leak probability.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The leak score (probability) is fed to the human analyst team, who further classify the leak and focus on the most important noise sources.</data>
      <data key="d10">4</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Feedback loop: about every six weeks, feedback from the field (e.g. false positives) is used to further classify and retrain the model.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">Mueller Water Products - Water Intelligence Platform (Acoustic Leak Detection)</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2e3vOxsHekE</data>
    <data key="d2">Water intelligence platform ingesting data from a large-scale IoT network of meters, smart fire hydrants, pressure monitoring and water quality devices. Daily acoustic data from fire hydrants is ingested through API Gateway, persisted in S3, batch processed in ECS to find correlations, and classified by a SageMaker ML endpoint to produce a leak probability for human analysts. Benefits: protection against non-revenue water loss and ecological protection. Future: heavier focus on ML and analytics and additional data sources such as flow and pressure.</data>
    <data key="d3">IoT, Machine Learning, Analytics</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
